\par
DETAILED COMMENTS (to be returned to the author(s))\par
[Reviewers are encouraged to provide substantitive comments \par
supporting their numerical scale ratings. Reviews without \par
such comments may not be considered in the overall rating of the paper.]\par
\par
This paper proposes a few operators for integrating traditional
and the content-based data management techniques.  I have at least
three major concerns about this paper.

1. Novelty?  When a fuel-efficient engine is designed,
   we say that the engine is a novel invention.  However, "inventing"
   a name for the engine can hardly be a scientific breakthrough.
   In this paper, the authors defined a few operators, and repeatedly
   (more than three times) claimed for novelty.  It is appropriate to
   replace "novel" with "new."

2. Is similarity-join really new?
   The authors did not cite/discuss the following related similarity-join 
   papers.  (The number of papers on similarity-join is not negligible, 
   and the authors could have found them easily via google.) The novelty 
   claim of this paper is thus in jeopardy.  The authors should perform 
   a survey on related work and argue what are new in this paper.

   [1] R. Agrawal, et. al. A Fast Algorithm for High-dimensional 
   Similarity Joins, ICDE, 1997.
   [2] J. Shafer, R. Agrawal. Parallel Algorithms for High-dimensional 
   Proximity Joins, VLDB 1997.
   
3. Should similarity be symmetric?
   A major contribution (the only thing "new" that I can identify in 
   this work) that the authors claimed is that they made
   similarity-join symmetric, and the resulting commutative property 
   made query optimization feasible.  Unfortunately, this is precisely 
   the wrong thing to do for content-based image retrieval.  It has been
   well known in the cognitive psychology community that perceptual
   similarity is non-metric.  The CBIR community has just begun 
   to seriously face/treat this issue.  Performing query optimization on
   the assumption that similarity is symmetric will lead to erroneous 
   results.

